{# ==================== THE HOUSE ==================== #}
{#
  Brand-level, not founder-level. The brief for this page was to use
  CLIENT_FACTS.md's approved origin story directly, and that story is already
  fully deployed above: its first paragraph is spread across chapters 01 to 05
  almost verbatim, and its second is chapter 06. There was no unused approved
  prose left to add, so this section is built from the other cleared source,
  CLIENT_FACTS.md's "The differentiators (three tiers)", which exists to be
  used "whenever the craft claim needs support."

  Every fact below traces. Tier 1: own Noodle Room, machine imported from
  Japan, broth simmered every morning in the Crown Point commissary and driven
  to every San Diego location. Tier 2: trained through Japanese kitchens
  including Matsuhisa, took over Convoy in 2001, still runs the business.
  Tier 3: walk-in only, no reservations, six neighborhoods, and service named
  in reviews more often than food (131 of 154).

  CLIENT_FACTS.md's own instruction on ordering is followed: "Tier 1 is the
  news. Tier 2 is why it is credible. Tier 3 is why people come back. Do not
  lead with Tier 3 and do not let Tier 2 turn into a founder-worship page."

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CLAIMED. No cadence on the noodles, only on the
  broth (Open Decision #3). No "every location" noodle claim (#2). No employee
  count, which CLIENT_FACTS.md marks an estimate. No review rating, which is a
  live number. The 131 of 154 figure is a count from the five-star sample and
  is written as what reviews mention, not as a rating.
#}
<section class="sect sect--rule" aria-labelledby="house-title">
  <div class="sect__inner">
    <header class="sect__head">
      <span class="sect__eyebrow">The house</span>
      <h2 class="sect__title" id="house-title">What Tajima <em>runs.</em></h2>
    </header>

    <div class="bento">

      <article class="cell diff__cell reveal">
        <h3 class="diff__title">Our own <em>room.</em></h3>
        <p class="diff__body">
          The noodles are made in our own Noodle Room, on a machine that came over from Japan. The broth is simmered every morning in our own commissary in Crown Point and driven out to every San Diego location before service. Most ramen shops buy both. <a class="sect__link" href="/noodle-room/">See the room</a>.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="cell diff__cell reveal">
        <h3 class="diff__title">One owner, <em>since 2001.</em></h3>
        <p class="diff__body">
          Sam worked his way through Japanese kitchens, including a stretch at Matsuhisa under Nobu Matsuhisa, before taking over the room on Convoy Street in 2001. He still runs the business. Twenty-five years in San Diego, not twenty-five months.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="cell diff__cell reveal">
        <h3 class="diff__title">Six rooms. <em>Walk in.</em></h3>
        <p class="diff__body">
          Six neighborhoods, each with its own character. No reservations, no queue theater. The crew gets named in reviews more often than the food does, and that is produced by the floor, not by the marketing. <a class="sect__link" href="/locations/">Find your Tajima</a>.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

